[26f7ced | 2026-07-14 11:43:21 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Make coverage near-free: raw Coverage dumps, merge once

    The coverage run took ~24s longer than `rake bacon` (36s) even after clearing
    the stale resultset. Cause: each of the ~72 spec processes ran SimpleCov's full
    at_exit result-processing and merged into a shared resultset file that grew as
    the run progressed (O(n^2)). Measured: Ruby's stdlib Coverage adds no
    detectable per-process overhead (0.080s vs 0.087s bare load), so the entire
    delta was SimpleCov machinery, not the coverage instrumentation itself.
    
    Each spec process now tracks with Coverage.start(lines: true) and dumps its raw
    result to coverage/raw/<pid>.dump. tasks/coverage.rake unions the dumps and
    does a single SimpleCov pass (add_not_loaded_files reproduces track_files 0%
    files; add_filter drops specs), preserving the exact report and HTML.
    
    Result: identical numbers (90.57%, 3092/3414, 107 files, 1 at 0%), full run
    down from ~61s to ~39s -- the coverage delta over `rake bacon` is now ~2s.

[79db884 | 2026-07-14 11:33:53 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Clear coverage resultset before each run

    SimpleCov merges every process's result into coverage/.resultset.json and
    keeps prior runs' entries for merge_timeout (24h, set in spec/helper.rb).
    Nothing cleared it between runs (CLEAN.include('') was a no-op globbing the
    empty string), so the file accumulated 500+ command_names and grew to ~97MB.
    Every one of the ~72 spec processes then re-parsed and rewrote it at exit:
    measured 1.56s/process against the bloated file vs 0.22s against a fresh one,
    ~95s of pure waste per run and growing.
    
    rm the coverage dir at the start of the task, and fix the CLEAN glob to point
    at coverage/. Run time is now a stable ~61s (down from ~2.5min and climbing);
    resultset holds 72 entries / 14MB as intended.

[6d23f15 | 2026-07-14 11:25:05 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Fix heap overflow in EvalResult.to_dict search cursor

    Tcl_DictObjFirst fills a full Tcl_DictSearch struct (24 bytes on LP64), but
    to_dict allocated a bare MemoryPointer(:pointer) of 8 bytes, so Tcl wrote 16
    bytes past the buffer into adjacent heap on every dict->Hash conversion. The
    corruption was silent (results stayed correct), which is why no spec caught it.
    
    Add a properly-sized TclDictSearch FFI struct (FFI computes size/alignment per
    platform) and allocate it in to_dict.

