[e8149e5 | 2026-07-06 14:22:49 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Run specs headless on one private Xvfb + openbox

    The bacon and coverage tasks spawned one ruby process per spec file, each
    creating a mapped Tk root on the ambient $DISPLAY, so a full run popped ~61
    windows on the desktop.
    
    Add tasks/support/headless_display.rb: it starts a single private Xvfb (via
    -displayfd, so it picks a free display and signals readiness with no lockfile
    race) plus one openbox for the whole run, and passes DISPLAY to every spec
    subprocess. A window manager is mandatory, not optional -- the bind/event specs
    synthesise key events that are only delivered to a window with real keyboard
    focus, which bare Xvfb never grants; the helper polls xprop
    _NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK so the first spec cannot race WM startup. Both
    processes are torn down in ensure. TK_SPEC_DISPLAY=:0 restores the old
    visible-on-desktop behaviour.
    
    Xvfb/openbox chatter (harmless xkbcomp F23/F24 keymap warnings from
    xkeyboard-config, socket-probe noise, openbox default-config messages) is
    redirected to tmp/xserver.log -- persisted and announced, not discarded, so
    real server errors stay diagnosable.
    
    flake.nix gains xorg-server, openbox and xprop on Linux; the "headless is a
    deliberate CI concern" comment is replaced accordingly.

[8c8568b | 2026-07-06 10:50:27 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Return command results from button-family invoke; fix block wiring

    Button, CheckButton and RadioButton#invoke used execute_only since the
    original callback-bridge work, discarding the -command result that
    their own docstrings promise ('the return value is the return value
    from the Tcl command'). All three now use execute and return the Ruby
    callback's value marshalled through the Tcl result (nil without a
    command), with round-trip specs for each.
    
    Writing the radiobutton spec exposed a latent initializer bug:
    Button#initialize registered a given block's command into a local
    options hash before calling super, so Widget#initialize's
    hash-as-parent shift discarded it — Button.new(options) { block }
    silently lost the command and leaked the registered callback. It now
    registers through Widget#initialize's options yield. That made
    CheckButton's identical override redundant — previously it only worked
    because the two bugs cancelled: Button dropped its misregistered
    command and Widget's yield then registered the right one. The override
    is deleted; CheckButton inherits the fixed version.

[d757661 | 2026-07-06 10:41:34 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Restore Menu#invoke result now that callback results are safe

    Menu#invoke was switched to execute_only as a workaround for the
    callback-result segfault, discarding the invoke result. With
    obj_result= fixed to build real Tcl_Objs, retrieving the result is
    safe and meaningful again: invoke returns the entry command's result —
    for a Ruby proc entry, the proc's own return value marshalled through
    the Tcl result. The menu spec now asserts that round-trip, and the
    stale segfault reference in the event spec comment is dropped.
    
    Button/CheckButton/RadioButton#invoke keep their execute_only from the
    original callback-bridge design — they predate the workaround and were
    never changed for it.

[0de2c70 | 2026-07-06 10:36:16 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Fix segfault when Ruby callbacks return non-primitive values

    Interp#obj_result= handed Tcl_SetObjResult a Ruby String (the to_tcl
    command-embedding form) for any callback return value that was not a
    String, Integer, boolean, or Exception. FFI coerces the String to a
    raw byte pointer, Tcl dereferences those bytes as a Tcl_Obj, and the
    process segfaults in Tcl_GetStringFromObj when the result is next
    retrieved — reliably reproducible with a callback returning nil. This
    was previously misdiagnosed as a callback re-entrancy problem; entries
    without a Ruby proc were unaffected because obj_result= never ran.
    
    Every branch now builds a real Tcl_Obj (new ruby_to_tcl_obj): nil maps
    to the empty result, Symbols to their string, Integers go through the
    newly attached Tcl_NewWideIntObj (the old Tcl_NewIntObj silently
    truncated results above 32 bits), Floats through Tcl_NewDoubleObj, and
    Arrays recursively become genuine Tcl list objects, so lists round-trip
    typed instead of as a braced string. spec/ffi-tk/callback.rb pins the
    full matrix, including the exception error path.

[a999f24 | 2026-07-06 10:31:17 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Make single-option configure queries return the current value

    Settle the query contract: passing a Symbol/String option to a
    configure-style method returns that option's current value, matching
    cget semantics.
    
    Previously the form was broken everywhere it mattered:
    Grid.columnconfigure/rowconfigure crashed (Symbol has no
    to_tcl_options?), and Configure.common pushed the classic
    [option, dbname, dbclass, default, current] describe-tuple through
    tcl_options_to_hash, which mis-sliced it into garbage pairs — or
    crashed on Integer keys.
    
    Configure.common now recognises the describe-tuple by shape (five
    elements, first equals the queried option — class alone cannot
    distinguish it from ttk options whose bare value is itself a list,
    e.g. treeview item values) and extracts the current value; canvas
    itemconfigure answers with a string-typed tuple, extracted via lindex.
    ttk bare values pass through untouched. Grid gains the single-option
    branch and returns Tcl's native scalar.
    
    Specs cover grid column/row single-option queries, panedwindow
    describe-tuple extraction, and canvas string-tuple extraction.

[0d6f8a7 | 2026-07-06 10:15:36 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Deduplicate tile scrollbar orient/command; fix bare instantiation

    Tile::Scrollbar duplicated the orient and command methods it now
    inherits from Tk::Scrollbar (added there for the Scrollable helper
    fix); the inherited versions dispatch through the widget's own
    configure/cget, so the copies were redundant. Removing them exposed
    that a bare Tk::Tile::Scrollbar.new raised NoMethodError: initialize
    calls scrollbar_default, which only the YScrollbar/XScrollbar presets
    defined. The base class now provides it as a no-op hook, keeping ttk's
    default orientation, with specs covering bare instantiation and the
    inherited orient round-trip.

[7a1ad57 | 2026-07-06 10:09:26 UTC] Michael Fellinger <michael.fellinger@iohk.io>

  * Fix int64 conversion, bytearray results, and scrollbar linkage

    Three tracked correctness bugs, each with specs proving the fix:
    
    - EvalResult#to_int converted through a 32-bit pointer: 2**31 silently
      wrapped negative and larger values raised from Tcl_GetIntFromObj.
      Attach Tcl_GetWideIntFromObj and convert through :int64; 2**63-1 now
      round-trips exactly. The characterization specs that pinned the
      wrapping behavior are replaced with correct-value assertions.
    - The result guesser had no :bytearray branch, raising 'Unknown type'
      for any binary result. Attach Tcl_GetByteArrayFromObj and return the
      raw bytes as an ASCII-8BIT string — get_string_from_obj cannot be
      used here because it re-encodes NUL and high bytes as Tcl modified
      UTF-8, corrupting the data.
    - Scrollable#yscrollbar/#xscrollbar called Scrollbar#orient, which did
      not exist, so attaching a scrollbar to a scrollable widget raised
      NoMethodError. Add orient and command to Tk::Scrollbar (mirroring
      the tile scrollbar) and prove the scrollcommand linkage end-to-end:
      scrolling the listbox moves scrollbar.get. Delete yview_pickplace,
      which wrapped a subcommand removed in Tk 8.6.

